# Throwaway remediation plans (architecture review 2026-08-12)
plans/

# Replay-harness dev/test corpus slices (Plan 06) -- large, regenerate with
# services/flink-job/tools/cut_dev_slice.py from ~/stream-scout-corpus/
services/flink-job/corpus/

# Captured Kafka slices from the spec 004 measurement harnesses -- ~63 MB in
# phase5/ (mostly one post-cutover.jsonl) and ~5 MB in phase0/. These are the
# blobs that must never reach history; one `git add -A` was all it took.
#
# Deliberately NOT ignoring the harness scripts beside them. phase5/driver.py
# is what produced the SC-001, SC-002, SC-004 and SC-006 numbers in
# specs/004-eventsub-parallel-reconciler/research.md, and excluding the whole
# directory would leave those unreproducible by anyone else. The scripts are
# throwaway, but they should stay addable.
services/stream-monitoring/phase0/*.jsonl
services/stream-monitoring/phase5/*.jsonl
# Regenerable measurement output from those scripts.
services/stream-monitoring/phase5/*.json

# Local working notes, never meant to be tracked. The file has said so since
# 2026-08-24 and also recorded that nothing enforced it -- it sat one
# `git add -A` from being committed, and it carries token-file permission
# internals and host paths. This is the rule it asked for. Anchored, so a
# future services/*/KNOWN_ISSUES.md is not silently ignored too.
#
# Tension worth knowing about, and older than this rule: several tracked files
# cite this one as the record of record, including a log line the Flink job
# emits to operators (clip_detector_job.py, "See KNOWN_ISSUES.md Issue 3
# follow-up"). It has never been committed on any ref, so those references
# were already dangling in a fresh clone -- this rule makes the situation
# explicit rather than creating it. The file's own header says the fix is to
# promote anything that must outlive this machine into a tracked doc.
/KNOWN_ISSUES.md

# Agent-created git worktrees (full duplicate checkouts)
.claude/worktrees/

# Python wheels. A venv build in the repo root once swept 16 of these
# (~4 MB) into a commit via `git add -A`. Nothing here installs from a
# local wheel: requirements.txt pins from PyPI.
*.whl
